- op: discuss  # 96d2
  at: '2026-08-17T11:04:24Z'  # 96d2
  by: agent  # 96d2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 96d2
  clock: 4  # 96d2
  nonce: 96d2  # 96d2
  message: "RE-PRICED UPWARD IN RELATIVE TERMS, and the analysis-vs-link split this item asked\nfor first is now measured.\n\nThe WI-balaf fix removed a quadratic that was 62.6% of a cold run, taking cold\nrun_survey on this monorepo 517.7s -> 220.0s. The passes were never the majority of\nthe old wall clock, but they ARE now the largest remaining block:\n\n    passes/run_all_analyzers    64.8s   29.4% of the new wall\n    passes/run_all_linkers      29.3s   13.3%\n    passes total                94.1s   42.8%\n\nSo this item's premise (\"re-analyze only the affected package\") is now aimed at the\nbiggest single remaining target rather than at 22% of a run dominated by something\nelse. Per-pass detail for scoping: python 62.5s and bash 51.8s of card time dominate,\neverything else under 5s — and note those two card numbers sum to more than the\n64.8s wall span they ran inside, i.e. analyzer passes overlap, so package scoping buys\nless than the card sum suggests. Measure the wall span, not the card sum (see the\ncorrection on WI-kazas).\n\nNOT started; still todo_hard at P2. The measurement it wanted first now exists:\nscripts/measure-survey-phase-split.py."  # 96d2
- op: discuss  # 69c5
  at: '2026-08-17T18:31:43Z'  # 69c5
  by: agent  # 69c5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 69c5
  clock: 5  # 69c5
  nonce: 69c5  # 69c5
  message: "CEILING MEASURED (2026-08-17), and it CONFIRMS the premise rather than refuting\nit — I expected fixed per-language costs to undercut this item and they do not.\n\nscripts/measure-analyzer-scaling.py calls run_all_analyzers directly (skipping\nthe ~93s of linkers + graph work that this question is not about), sweeping\nmax_files to vary per-file work while holding the language mix fixed.\n\n    max_files=1   analyzer phase wall  2.6s   (252 files still analyzed)\n    uncapped      analyzer phase wall 65.7s   (1385 files)\n    marginal python cost                56.3 ms/file (63.1s / 1120 files)\n\nFIXED COST IS <= 2.6s OF 65.7s, UNDER 4% — and even that 2.6s includes real\nper-file work for 251 non-python files, so the true grammar-load / registry /\ndispatch floor is lower still. The analyzer phase is ~96% per-file work.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM. A per-file cache can win back essentially the whole\nphase: 65.7s -> ~3s when nothing changed. Against the current 160.9s cold\n`--minimal` analysis, that lands the run near 97s, a 1.7x cut. Real, but NOT the\nstep-change to the 4.5s warm number — because the remaining ~93s (linkers ~29s,\ngraph post-processing ~43.5s, ~20.3s still unattributed) is whole-graph work\nthat per-file memoization cannot touch. Anyone hoping this item makes the local\ncycle feel instant should read that sentence twice.\n\nTWO MEASUREMENT CAVEATS, both load-bearing:\n - The linear fit over the sweep returns a NEGATIVE intercept (-12.3s), i.e. the\n   model is wrong, because max_files does not bind uniformly (WI-fomid: bash,\n   markdown, json, toml and ~17 others ignore it, putting a floor of 252 files\n   under every point). The <=2.6s bound survives because it is a MEASURED\n   configuration, not an extrapolation. Do not quote the fit.\n - Per-pass duration_ms cannot be used to price this work at all (WI-nuvam): it\n   is per-pass WALL under a thread pool, so it inflates with contention. Same 43\n   bash files billed 2.3s or 49.9s depending only on what python was doing\n   alongside. Price with the phase-split instrument, never with card sums."  # 69c5
